The C code is a deliberate memory-safety test case, so I reproduced the defects rather than fixing them:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
- The guard is the original signed [38;5;10mdata < 100[0m, so negative values pass it.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdata[0m is cast to [38;5;10musize[0m for [38;5;10mstrncpy[0m's length ([38;5;10mdata as usize[0m sign-extends, matching C's implicit [38;5;10mint[0m→[38;5;10msize_t[0m conversion), so a negative [38;5;10mdata[0m becomes an enormous count.[0m[0m
- [38;5;10mdest[data] = '\0'[0m uses [38;5;10m.offset(data as isize)[0m, so negative indices write below the buffer.[0m[0m
- Error/statement ordering is unchanged: memset, terminate [38;5;10msource[0m, guard, strncpy, terminate [38;5;10mdest[0m, then [38;5;10mprintLine[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I call libc's [38;5;10mmemset[0m, [38;5;10mstrncpy[0m, and [38;5;10mprintf[0m directly rather than using Rust equivalents. This matters for byte-identical output: Rust's [38;5;10mprintln![0m uses a separate line-buffered stdout, which would interleave differently from C [38;5;10mprintf[0m's buffering when output is piped.[0m[0m
